  "response": "Ran `crimes context src/billing.ts` — 8 findings on the file (1 high, 5 medium, 2 low), plus two `contract_drift` findings filed against `src/api/state.ts` that name `src/billing.ts` as the other side. I also read the file to confirm each finding is real rather than a heuristic misfire.\n\nOne thing that reframes everything below: `src/billing.test.ts` — the file `crimes context` lists under \"Likely tests\" — **never imports `src/billing.ts`**. It defines local stubs `renderEnterprisePlan()` and `createInvoice()` and tests those. That's what the two `weak_test_signal` findings (`crime_00025`, zero assertions; `crime_00031`, one weak matcher) are pointing at from below. So the file has *no* real test coverage, which matches the `test gap: bottom-quartile` clue and sets the safe order of operations.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Fix the refund cluster at lines 33–44 (three findings, twelve lines)\n\nThree separate charges stack on one tiny region, and together they're the most likely thing to make an agent write a wrong change:\n\n- **False Identity** (`name_behavior_mismatch::src/billing.ts::calculateRefundPreview`, conf 0.80) — `calculateRefundPreview` is named like a pure computation but awaits `saveRefundAudit` and `sendRefundEmail` before returning `invoice.totalCents`. Anything that \"previews\" gets called speculatively — on hover, in a loop, twice under React strict mode — and each call emails the customer.\n- **Logic in the Alibi** (`logic_in_comments`, conf 0.71) — line 39 says *\"Only owners can refund enterprise plans unless billing support approves.\"* The evidence line \"domain terms not found nearby: owner, plan, billing\" is correct: the body reads `user.id`, `user.email`, `invoice.id` and **never inspects `user.plan` or any role**. The authorization rule exists only as prose. An agent that reads the comment will assume the guard is enforced somewhere and won't add it.\n- **Commented-Out Corpse** (`commented_out_code`, conf 0.90) — lines 33–37 are a disabled refund flow (`loadRefund` → `approveRefund` → `sendRefundReceipt`) sitting immediately above the live function. It's the nearest \"example\" an agent has of how refunds work, and it's stale.\n\n**Change:** rename to `issueRefundPreviewAndNotify` (or split the pure `invoice.totalCents` read out from the audit+email side effects — the finding's own `rename_or_extract_side_effect` action), lift the prose rule into a real guard (`assertCanRefund(user, invoice)` that checks plan and role and throws), and delete lines 33–37 — git has them.\n\nI'm putting this first even though `crimes` ranks `direct_date` above it on aggregate risk. Its findings are individually mid-severity, but they're co-located, cheap to fix, and the failure mode is customer-visible (unauthorized refunds, duplicate emails) rather than internal.\n\n## 2. Split `generateInvoice` into pure helpers (lines 50–253)\n\n`large_function::src/billing.ts::generateInvoice` — severity 0.90, confidence 0.95, 204 lines against a 60-line domain threshold (3.4×). It's the single highest-severity finding on the file, and reading it confirms the charge isn't just line-counting: the body's own section comments enumerate eight distinct responsibilities — identity/metadata, plan pricing, loyalty discount, promo resolution, fraud scoring, tax, currency, persistence shape, PII audit log, metrics, email rendering, retry loop.\n\nTwo of those regions carry their own findings:\n\n- **Catch and Release** (`swallowed_error`, line 245) — the retry loop's `catch {}` has no binding at all; three attempts can fail and the caller gets a successful `Invoice` back with `metadata.emailAttempts = \"3\"` as the only trace.\n- The tax ladder at lines 146–154 is byte-identical to the exported `tax()` at 257–268. `crimes` doesn't flag this pair (both copies are inline if/else chains, not extracted blocks), but the comment at 255 admits it, and it's a live split-brain: change a VAT rate and one copy silently keeps the old one.\n\n**Change:** extract `priceLines`, `loyaltyDiscount`, `applyPromo`, `scoreRisk`, `currencyFor`, `renderInvoiceEmail` as pure functions, have the invoice path call the existing `tax()` instead of its inline duplicate, and leave `generateInvoice` as a thin orchestrator. Give the catch a binding and either rethrow or return a typed `emailFailed` result. This also collapses `large_file` (`crime_00037`, 311 lines vs a 300 threshold) as a side effect — the God File finding is really just this function overflowing.\n\n## 3. Inject a clock, and make the tests real\n\n`direct_date::src/billing.ts` — 7 uses at lines 57, 63, 92, 198, 238, 250, 273. `crimes` ranks this the file's top finding by aggregate risk, and the reason it matters here isn't determinism-in-the-abstract: **`Date.now()` at line 92 and line 273 decides money.** `daysSince` drives the loyalty discount tiers (>365 → 10%, >180 → 5%, >90 → 2%) and every branch of `recommendUpgrade`. Today there is no way to test the 365-day boundary without monkey-patching global time.\n\n**Change:** take `now: () => number` (or a `Clock`) as a parameter through the domain boundary — the finding's `inject_clock` action — and then write the tests that `billing.test.ts` currently only pretends to have: actually import `generateInvoice`, pin the clock, and assert the discount tier boundaries and the tax/currency table.\n\nThis is third by severity but it's the **enabler** — with a pinned clock and real assertions you can do #2 as a verified refactor instead of a hopeful one. If you're doing all three, the order is 1 → 3 → 2.\n\n---\n\nWorth knowing but outside the top 3: `contract_drift` (`crime_00033`, severity 0.96) says this file's `Invoice` (11 fields, line 19) and `src/api/state.ts`'s `Invoice` (2 fields) describe the same record and disagree on six — including `currency`, `taxCents`, `subtotalCents`, and `userId`. Same story for `User` (`crime_00032`). Nothing in the build checks them against each other. It's high-severity but it's a cross-file fix that belongs in a change of its own, and touching it while `generateInvoice` is still 204 lines means re-reading the whole thing twice.",
